<span id="freeTextContainer1459700177648584352">Good in theory but not put into practice well.<br/><br/>Radiant has some fascinating magic and lore and an interesting world behind it all that feels a bit like a combination Mashima (<a href='https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2454986.Fairy_Tail__Vol__01__Fairy_Tail___1_' title='Fairy Tail, Vol. 01 (Fairy Tail, #1) by Hiro Mashima' rel='nofollow noopener'>Fairy Tail</a>) meets Kato (<a href='https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/9876989.Blue_Exorcist__Vol__1__Blue_Exorcist___1_' title='Blue Exorcist, Vol. 1 (Blue Exorcist, #1) by Kazue Kato' rel='nofollow noopener'>Blue Exorcist</a>). The problem comes with the execution. There is <i>a lot</i> packed into this first volume with a lot of telling rather than showing. I felt my eyes glaze over a few times and I started skimming large portions of text because there were walls of word bubbles to explain a situation rather than </span>
  <span id="freeText1459700177648584352" style="display:none">Good in theory but not put into practice well.<br /><br />Radiant has some fascinating magic and lore and an interesting world behind it all that feels a bit like a combination Mashima (<a href="https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2454986.Fairy_Tail__Vol__01__Fairy_Tail___1_" title="Fairy Tail, Vol. 01 (Fairy Tail, #1) by Hiro Mashima" rel="nofollow noopener">Fairy Tail</a>) meets Kato (<a href="https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/9876989.Blue_Exorcist__Vol__1__Blue_Exorcist___1_" title="Blue Exorcist, Vol. 1 (Blue Exorcist, #1) by Kazue Kato" rel="nofollow noopener">Blue Exorcist</a>). The problem comes with the execution. There is <i>a lot</i> packed into this first volume with a lot of telling rather than showing. I felt my eyes glaze over a few times and I started skimming large portions of text because there were walls of word bubbles to explain a situation rather than just running with it and having us understand based on the scene. Yes, it's a big world with a weird magic system and some even weirder lore behind it. It doesn't mean we need ALL of that packed into one chapter.<br /><br />The story itself moves at a decent pace though, and so the mass quantities of exposition for each event gets crammed into a few panels. Radiant also suffers from characters talking way too much about things that aren't really important. Sometimes it builds up to a joke (like the "beware the yellow cat" stuff), sometimes it's exposition being shouted to people who already would know this stuff, and sometimes it just... builds to nothing? Maybe it was supposed to be character building or something.<br /><br />I know it sounds like I'm just complaining, but that's because if not for these big flaws, this would work <i>great</i>. Characters are interesting, the art is expressive, character designs are neat with a touch of weird that makes it fun, the backgrounds are detailed, particularly when they get to Artemis. I especially liked the chapter page in Artemis, where you saw the busy streets and the Chapter 4 header and title are cleverly incorporated into the art.<br /><br />The story of a boy with weird powers, even in a world full of weird powers, might be a cliche formula, but it's become cliche because it <i>works</i>. In this case, Seth is a boy who can cast magic without the usual incantations or magic tools. He's powerful, but not too bright and prone to causing mass destruction. He has an "infection" (a side effect from touching a nemesis and not dying) in the form of the horns on his head (and possibly the fact he can use magic barehanded). He takes down his first nemesis and kicks the butts of a gang of "bad" wizards, earning him the love of the town he saved and the confidence to go track down the home of all the nemeses, the titular "Radiant." He'll probably go through a training arc of some sort in Artemis and have to dodge the inquisition (an institution that isn't really well explained despite the lengthy explanations of everything else).<br /><br />I kind of want to continue this series, but on the other hand, the messy world building via extended info dumps is something that's normally a deal breaker for me in manga. It's both familiar and a little off beat and in theory something I would normally really like. I think I'll give the next volume a shot and see if things even out a bit more as it goes.</span>
