<span id="freeTextContainer3453651866796187497">This manga started with a pretty good first page, all the colors, all the wizards on their brooms ready to fight the Nemesis in the distance. Albeit these people down there don´t look particularly frightened.&quot;<br/>And here is our protagonist, a teenage idiot named Seth who cannot differentiate between a herd of peaceful cattle and giant city destroying monsters. Alma (his teacher) got a point: with all the destruction Seth caused with his recklessness, what makes him then different from the Nemesis? </span>
  <span id="freeText3453651866796187497" style="display:none">This manga started with a pretty good first page, all the colors, all the wizards on their brooms ready to fight the Nemesis in the distance. Albeit these people down there don´t look particularly frightened."<br />And here is our protagonist, a teenage idiot named Seth who cannot differentiate between a herd of peaceful cattle and giant city destroying monsters. Alma (his teacher) got a point: with all the destruction Seth caused with his recklessness, what makes him then different from the Nemesis? For all they now, the Nemesis would believe to be acting out of just cause as well.<br />And that Alma can handle assholes like this merchant who rather destroys his merchandice than selling it to her (a witch), speaks volumes. Albeit to be clear, is it a good idea to play into the stereotypes that he has? And there is something I don´t get: if wizards are the only ones who can destroy the Nemesis, why are they so feared and without government help? <br />The first other wizards we meet here is the Bravey Quartett who do seem both competent as Nemesis fighters and goofy at the same time. I would have taken them for wizards for hire. Granted, the twist reveals them as thieves, but still, it was good writing.<br />And while it is explained where wizards come from, what the Nemesis infection does to them, what they do to use Fantasia (aka magic) and fight Nemesis. I just wonder, if wizards come into being by basically surviving an encounter with a Nemesis and it happens with such a low percentage, how many people have been killed already to have wizards be something so widely known and feared?<br />And the boss of the Quartett is right, not only did the townsfolk hate Seth, but he unleashed the Nemesis by being so eager to fight one he cracked its egg and then their fight destroyed much of the town. So even if the townpeople weren´t bigotted, they would have plenty of reasons to hate him.<br />Something odd about the writing: If Seth can cast spells without his gloves or something, why didn´t he do so before? He is such an impulsive idiot that it is hard to believe he wouldn't have done so already. Still, as much as an impulsive idiot as he is, at least he has morals and won´t let Alma take the blame for him. When he later goes to the magical school, you can see what an idiot he is. Well, at least it in character for him to remember that Alma told him not to trust a yellow cat when it is already too late. Sure, she could have been more specific, but considered that we are talking about Seth here, I doubt he would have remembered any of it. I really wonder how he wants to find the Nemesis nest (aka the Radiant) now.<br />Suddenly we are done with the story. Look at that, Seth is apparently not the only horned wizard (and boy did some others have weird infections) and apparently the other/s could control Fantasia as well without tools.</span>
